:"/docs/cfc-budget#who-was-using-more-care","content":"**Personal-care growth** — children whose parents may now be paid."},"33":{"id":"/docs/cfc-budget-18","page_id":"/docs/cfc-budget","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cfc-budget#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"This is the core “why is HCBS in the news?” story: a program sold partly as a General Fund saver became a General Fund spender when real-world use outran the forecast."},"34":{"id":"/docs/cfc-budget-19","page_id":"/docs/cfc-budget","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cfc-budget#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"A provider-side response is not “deny the cost.” It is “fix oversight, admin complexity, and proportional rules before defaulting to cuts.” See Why this matters."},"35":{"id":"/docs/cfc-budget-20","page_id":"/docs/cfc-budget","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cfc-budget#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Next: Peer & support services →"},"36":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"page","content":"Concepts to know first","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first"},"37":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-0","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first","content":"Plain-English explanations of terms used in the Sept 17 briefing"},"38":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-1","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#money-language","content":"Money language"},"39":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-2","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#general-fund","content":"General Fund"},"40":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-3","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#federal-match-also-called-fmap","content":"Federal match (also called FMAP)"},"41":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-4","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#fiscal-year-fy","content":"Fiscal year (FY)"},"42":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-5","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#utilization","content":"Utilization"},"43":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-6","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#who-delivers-the-care","content":"Who delivers the care"},"44":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-7","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#agency-delivered-care","content":"Agency-delivered care"},"45":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-8","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#paid-family-caregiving","content":"Paid family caregiving"},"46":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-9","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Homemaker vs. personal care (quick)"},"47":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-10","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#waiver-access","content":"Waiver access"},"48":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-11","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#oversight-language","content":"Oversight language"},"49":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-12","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#clinical-oversight","content":"Clinical oversight"},"50":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-13","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#case-management","content":"Case management"},"51":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-14","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#cost-shifting","content":"Cost shifting"},"52":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-15","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"How this maps to the highlights"},"53":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-16","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first","content":"Read this before the highlight pages. These ideas show up constantly in the Commission materials. Dotted underlines elsewhere also open the same definitions on hover / tap."},"54":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-17","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#general-fund","content":"Colorado’s main **state tax-supported** budget pot — money from state taxes and similar state revenue, **not** the federal share of Medicaid."},"55":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-18","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#general-fund","content":"When politicians say “this is costing the General Fund,” they mean it is hitting **Colorado’s own dollars**, which compete with schools, roads, prisons, and everything else in the state budget."},"56":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-19","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#general-fund","content":"Medicaid is usually paid with a mix of **state money + federal money**. The General Fund is the state side people worry about."},"57":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-20","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#federal-match-also-called-fmap","content":"The share of a Medicaid dollar the **federal government** pays. The rest is state."},"58":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-21","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#federal-match-also-called-fmap","content":"Example: if the federal match for a service is 50%, every $1 of service costs Colorado \\~$0.50 from the General Fund (simplified)."},"59":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-22","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#federal-match-also-called-fmap","content":"Some programs get an **enhanced** (higher) federal match — meaning Colorado’s share is smaller *per dollar of service*. That is why CFC was projected to *save* General Fund money: more federal dollars covering the same or similar care. If people use far more services than forecast, the state still pays its share on every extra hour — and the “savings” evaporate."},"60":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-23","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#fiscal-year-fy","content":"Budget year, not calendar year. **FY2025–26** means the state budget year spanning mid-2025 into mid-2026 (Colorado’s fiscal year runs July–June). See also fiscal year."},"61":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-24","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#utilization","content":"How much of a service people actually use — hours, visits, or dollars billed — not just what is theoretically allowed. That is utilization."},"62":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-25","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#utilization","content":"Rising utilization = more service delivery and usually more spending, even if rates (price per hour) stay flat."},"63":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-26","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#agency-delivered-care","content":"Agency-delivered care: an agency like PPRS hires or contracts workers and sends them to support the member. The agency handles hiring, training, scheduling, billing, and compliance. The worker is not usually the member’s parent or relative (though agencies can employ family in some programs)."},"64":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-27","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#paid-family-caregiving","content":"Paid family caregiving: a **family member** (often a parent) is **paid** under Medicaid / HCBS rules to provide authorized supports — instead of (or in addition to) only unpaid family care."},"65":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-28","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#paid-family-caregiving","content":"This expanded under CFC. The Commission materials flag it because it can increase who gets paid and how many hours get billed, especially for personal care for children."},"66":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-29","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#paid-family-caregiving","content":"Neither model is “good” or “bad” by itself — they are different delivery models with different cost and oversight implications."},"67":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-30","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Service"},"68":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-31","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Rough idea"},"69":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-32","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Homemaker"},"70":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-33","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Help with the house — cleaning, laundry, light household tasks"},"71":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-34","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Personal care"},"72":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-35","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Help with the person’s body / daily living — bathing, dressing, meals, etc. (ADLs)"},"73":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-36","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#homemaker-vs-personal-care-quick","content":"Both can be CFC services. Both showed utilization growth in the worksheets."},"74":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-37","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#waiver-access","content":"A waiver is a Medicaid authority that lets someone qualify for certain HCBS. “Already had waiver access but had not been using the service” means: they were eligible before, but CFC made it easier or more attractive to actually start using homemaker hours."},"75":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-38","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#clinical-oversight","content":"Clinical oversight: professional / clinical supervision or review of whether a service is appropriate, documented, and delivered as intended. The worksheets say some peer/support services grew without enough of this."},"76":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-39","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#case-management","content":"Case management: a person or organization that coordinates a member’s plan, authorizations, and providers — the “middle layer” between the member and direct care. Not the same as the person who comes to your house to help with laundry."},"77":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-40","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#cost-shifting","content":"Cost shifting: cutting one service in a cheaper setting can push people into a **more expensive** setting (hospital, nursing facility, crisis care). Apparent “cuts” can fail to save General Fund money — or can cost more."},"78":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-41","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Concept"},"79":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-42","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Shows up most in"},"80":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-43","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"General Fund, federal match, utilization"},"81":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-44","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"CFC budget issue, Cut cautions"},"82":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-45","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Paid family caregiving, agency-delivered care"},"83":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-46","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"CFC budget issue"},"84":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-47","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Clinical oversight"},"85":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-48","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Peer & support services"},"86":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-49","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Case management / admin layer"},"87":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-50","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Admin spending"},"88":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-51","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Cost shifting"},"89":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-52","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Cut cautions"},"90":{"id":"/docs/concepts-first-53","page_id":"/docs/concepts-first","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/concepts-first#how-this-maps-to-the-highlights","content":"Next: CFC was a central budget issue →"},"91":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","type":"page","content":"6. Budget-cutting cautions","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission","Highlights"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/cut-cautions"},"92":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-0","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions","content":"Why apparent Medicaid cuts often do not save what people think"},"93":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-1","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cut-cautions#what-hcpf-cautioned","content":"What HCPF cautioned"},"94":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-2","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cut-cautions#plain-english","content":"Plain English"},"95":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-3","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/cut-cautions#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Why a newcomer should care"},"96":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-4","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions","content":"**Highlight 6 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."},"97":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-5","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#what-hcpf-cautioned","content":"HCPF cautioned that:"},"98":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-6","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#what-hcpf-cautioned","content":"Expenditure figures are **approximate ranges** — not precise estimates of savings from repealing legislation."},"99":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-7","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#what-hcpf-cautioned","content":"Some reductions can **move costs into more expensive settings** rather than produce true savings (cost shifting — e.g. less home support → more crisis / institutional care)."},"100":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-8","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#what-hcpf-cautioned","content":"Federal matching rates mean apparent cuts produce very different General Fund savings depending on which population is affected."},"101":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-9","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#plain-english","content":"“Cut $X of Medicaid services” does **not** automatically mean “save $X for Colorado.” The federal share, who is cut, and where people go next all change the math — sometimes cutting home care can cost the state more later."},"102":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-10","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"This is the official caution providers lean on: do not treat service reduction as the first or only lever. Fix complexity, oversight, and design first where possible."},"103":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-11","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Refresh money terms: Concepts to know first."},"104":{"id":"/docs/cut-cautions-12","page_id":"/docs/cut-cautions","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/cut-cautions#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Next: Why this matters →"},"105":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","type":"page","content":"5. Essential vs. broader supports","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission","Highlights"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader"},"106":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-0","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","content":"The worksheet exercise about what Medicaid should prioritize"},"107":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-1","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"What commissioners were asked"},"108":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-2","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#critical-framing-do-not-miss-this","content":"Critical framing (do not miss this)"},"109":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-3","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#plain-english","content":"Plain English"},"110":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-4","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Why a newcomer should care"},"111":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-5","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","content":"**Highlight 5 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."},"112":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-6","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"A second worksheet asked members to consider whether various Medicaid benefits primarily:"},"113":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-7","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"keep people **healthy and safe**,"},"114":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-8","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"support a **full life**,"},"115":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-9","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"do **both**, or"},"116":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-10","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"need **more information**."},"117":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-11","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#what-commissioners-were-asked","content":"Personal care and homemaker under CFC — including paid family caregiving — were specifically included in that exercise."},"118":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-12","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#critical-framing-do-not-miss-this","content":"The worksheet describes this as a **first reading** — **not** a decision and **not** a vote to cut any service."},"119":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-13","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#plain-english","content":"Policymakers are sorting benefits into “must have for safety” vs. “helps someone live a fuller life.” That sorting can precede later budget fights. Including CFC personal care / homemaker / paid family care means those services are in the conversation — but the Sept 17 materials were not themselves a cut vote."},"120":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-14","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"This is the political risk channel for PPRS-type services. Provider advocacy focuses on cost and quality solutions so “sort and cut” is not the only path."},"121":{"id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader-15","page_id":"/docs/essential-vs-broader","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/essential-vs-broader#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Next: Budget-cutting cautions →"},"122":{"id":"/docs/forecasting","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","type":"page","content":"3. Forecasting & design","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission","Highlights"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/forecasting"},"123":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-0","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting","content":"Why the original CFC cost projections missed reality"},"124":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-1","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/forecasting#what-happened","content":"What happened"},"125":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-2","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/forecasting#plain-english","content":"Plain English"},"126":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-3","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/forecasting#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Why a newcomer should care"},"127":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-4","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting","content":"**Highlight 3 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."},"128":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-5","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#what-happened","content":"HCPF materials said the original CFC forecast “did not sufficiently align policy and fiscal assumptions.”"},"129":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-6","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#what-happened","content":"The forecast also did **not** anticipate how providers would respond — creating a significant gap between projected and actual spending."},"130":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-7","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#plain-english","content":"When Colorado designed CFC, the budget math and the real-world program rules did not match well enough. Planners also underestimated how agencies and families would actually use the new flexibility. Result: surprise costs."},"131":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-8","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"This is why providers argue for **operational input before design changes** — people who run services day to day often see how utilization will behave before a spreadsheet does."},"132":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-9","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Background on CFC costs: CFC budget issue."},"133":{"id":"/docs/forecasting-10","page_id":"/docs/forecasting","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/forecasting#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Next: Admin & middle-layer spending →"},"134":{"id":"/docs","page_id":"/docs","type":"page","content":"Overview","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs"},"135":{"id":"/docs-0","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"What the Sept 17 Commission worksheets are and how to read this section"},"136":{"id":"/docs-1","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#what-this-section-is","content":"What this section is"},"137":{"id":"/docs-2","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#how-to-read-this","content":"How to read this"},"138":{"id":"/docs-3","page_id":"/docs","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs#one-sentence-big-picture","content":"One-sentence big picture"},"139":{"id":"/docs-4","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"**Document:** `Colorado_HCBS_LTSS_Sept17_2026_Highlights.docx`\\\n&#x2A;*Setting:** Colorado Commission on Medicaid — September 17, 2026\\\n&#x2A;*Nature:** A printable briefing based on the Commission’s official **member worksheets** — not a verbatim transcript of what people said out loud."},"140":{"id":"/docs-5","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs","content":"Dotted underlined words open a short definition on hover, focus, or tap."},"141":{"id":"/docs-6","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#what-this-section-is","content":"Colorado’s Medicaid commission reviewed written worksheets about spending growth in community-based long-term care. That pressure is why providers are being asked for concrete solutions — not only arguments for or against cuts."},"142":{"id":"/docs-7","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#what-this-section-is","content":"You do **not** need to absorb all six highlights at once. Read them one page at a time."},"143":{"id":"/docs-8","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#how-to-read-this","content":"Start with Concepts to know first — General Fund, federal match, paid family caregiving, etc."},"144":{"id":"/docs-9","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#how-to-read-this","content":"Then walk the six highlight pages (sidebar under this section)."},"145":{"id":"/docs-10","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#how-to-read-this","content":"Finish with Why this matters."},"146":{"id":"/docs-11","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#one-sentence-big-picture","content":"The worksheet attributes **$1.26 billion** of General Fund appropriation growth between **FY2021 and FY2026** to community-based long-term care. That dollar figure is why HCBS / LTSS is under political and budget pressure."},"147":{"id":"/docs-12","page_id":"/docs","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs#one-sentence-big-picture","content":"Next: Concepts to know first →"},"148":{"id":"/docs/peer-support","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","type":"page","content":"2. Peer & support services","breadcrumbs":["Sept 17 Commission","Highlights"],"tags":[],"url":"/docs/peer-support"},"149":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-0","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support","content":"Utilization growth and clinical oversight concerns"},"150":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-1","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/peer-support#what-happened","content":"What happened"},"151":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-2","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/peer-support#plain-english","content":"Plain English"},"152":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-3","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","type":"heading","tags":[],"url":"/docs/peer-support#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"Why a newcomer should care"},"153":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-4","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support","content":"**Highlight 2 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."},"154":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-5","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#what-happened","content":"Peer and support-type services drew specific scrutiny for how fast use and spending grew:"},"155":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-6","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#what-happened","content":"Utilization rose from **9.25 hours per person per year** in FY2022–23 to **24.75** in FY2025–26."},"156":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-7","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#what-happened","content":"One service code increased from **$8.8 million*&#x2A; to **$51.8 million**."},"157":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-8","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#what-happened","content":"HCPF’s review found these services were being delivered **without adequate clinical oversight** — meaning not enough professional review of whether delivery was appropriate and controlled."},"158":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-9","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#plain-english","content":"People were using many more hours of these supports, one billing category roughly **sextupled** in spend, and the state thinks supervision / oversight did not keep up."},"159":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-10","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"This is the “growth without guardrails” story. It feeds themes of program integrity, earlier problem detection, and real-time technology flags — not only rate cuts."},"160":{"id":"/docs/peer-support-11","page_id":"/docs/peer-support","tags":[],"type":"text","url":"/docs/peer-support#why-a-newcomer-should-care","content":"New terms? Concepts to know first."},"161":{"id":"/docs/peer-support