om the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."],[111,"**Highlight 5 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."],[96,"**Highlight 6 of 6** from the Sept 17 Commission worksheets."],[31,"**Homemaker growth** — adults who already had waiver access but had not been using homemaker before."],[32,"**Personal-care growth** — children whose parents may now be paid."],[14,"1. CFC budget issue"],[148,"2. Peer & support services"],[122,"3. Forecasting & design"],[1,"4. Admin & middle-layer spend"],[105,"5. Essential vs. broader supports"],[91,"6. Budget-cutting cautions"],[34,"A provider-side response is not “deny the cost.” It is “fix oversight, admin complexity, and proportional rules before defaulting to cuts.” See Why this matters."],[112,"A second worksheet asked members to consider whether various Medicaid benefits primarily:"],[74,"A waiver is a Medicaid authority that lets someone qualify for certain HCBS. “Already had waiver access but had not been using the service” means: they were eligible before, but CFC made it easier or more attractive to actually start using homemaker hours."],[87,"Admin spending"],[175,"Admin vs. direct care dollars"],[176,"Administrative sustainability; simplify duplication"],[29,"Agencies sending workers (the PPRS-style model)"],[28,"Agency-delivered care"],[44,"Agency-delivered care"],[63,"Agency-delivered care: an agency like PPRS hires or contracts workers and sends them to support the member. The agency handles hiring, training, scheduling, billing, and compliance. The worker is not usually the member’s parent or relative (though agencies can employ family in some programs)."],[180,"Are training, credentialing, documentation, and compliance requirements **proportionate** across comparable non-skilled services?"],[132,"Background on CFC costs: CFC budget issue."],[174,"Better data + operational input before design changes"],[73,"Both can be CFC services. Both showed utilization growth in the worksheets."],[60,"Budget year, not calendar year.