"Cost shifting"],[77,"Cost shifting: cutting one service in a cheaper setting can push people into a **more expensive** setting (hospital, nursing facility, crisis care). Apparent “cuts” can fail to save General Fund money — or can cost more."],[108,"Critical framing (do not miss this)"],[89,"Cut cautions"],[177,"Cut cautions / cost shifting"],[115,"do **both**, or"],[178,"Don’t cut services first — fix admin and oversight"],[140,"Dotted underlined words open a short definition on hover, focus, or tap."],[58,"Example: if the federal match for a service is 50%, every $1 of service costs Colorado \\~$0.50 from the General Fund (simplified)."],[98,"Expenditure figures are **approximate ranges** — not precise estimates of savings from repealing legislation."],[27,"Family members (e.g. parents) getting paid for authorized supports"],[40,"Federal match (also called FMAP)"],[100,"Federal matching rates mean apparent cuts produce very different General Fund savings depending on which population is affected."],[145,"Finish with Why this matters."],[41,"Fiscal year (FY)"],[173,"Forecasting failure"],[39,"General Fund"],[80,"General Fund, federal match, utilization"],[97,"HCPF cautioned that:"],[9,"HCPF materials did **not** identify this admin / middle layer as a driver of the spending growth being discussed. It was put on the table as a question — not as HCPF’s official explanation of the $1.26B growth."],[128,"HCPF materials said the original CFC forecast “did not sufficiently align policy and fiscal assumptions.”"],[157,"HCPF’s review found these services were being delivered **without adequate clinical oversight** — meaning not enough professional review of whether delivery was appropriate and controlled."],[70,"Help with the house — cleaning, laundry, light household tasks"],[72,"Help with the person’s body / daily living — bathing, dressing, meals, etc. (ADLs)"],[69,"Homemaker"],[46,"Homemaker vs. personal care (quick)"],[61,"How much of a service people actually use — 