ps"],[158,"People were using many more hours of these supports, one billing category roughly **sextupled** in spend, and the state thinks supervision / oversight did not keep up."],[71,"Personal care"],[117,"Personal care and homemaker under CFC — including paid family caregiving — were specifically included in that exercise."],[94,"Plain English"],[109,"Plain English"],[125,"Plain English"],[151,"Plain English"],[37,"Plain-English explanations of terms used in the Sept 17 briefing"],[119,"Policymakers are sorting benefits into “must have for safety” vs. “helps someone live a fuller life.” That sorting can precede later budget fights. Including CFC personal care / homemaker / paid family care means those services are in the conversation — but the Sept 17 materials were not themselves a cut vote."],[184,"Primary source: Colorado Commission on Medicaid, Member Worksheets, September 17, 2026, Worksheets 1–2. This section summarizes the **written worksheet**. Do not treat worksheet language as spoken quotations unless confirmed against the meeting recording."],[172,"Program integrity; real-time technology flags"],[170,"Proportionate requirements; quality provider infrastructure"],[168,"Provider response theme"],[164,"Questions the briefing raises for HCBS stakeholders"],[53,"Read this before the highlight pages. These ideas show up constantly in the Commission materials. Dotted underlines elsewhere also open the same definitions on hover / tap."],[103,"Refresh money terms: Concepts to know first."],[62,"Rising utilization = more service delivery and usually more spending, even if rates (price per hour) stay flat."],[68,"Rough idea"],[67,"Service"],[79,"Shows up most in"],[59,"Some programs get an **enhanced** (higher) federal match — meaning Colorado’s share is smaller *per dollar of service*. That is why CFC was projected to *save* General Fund money: more federal dollars covering the same or similar care. If people use far more services than forecast, the state still 